{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>and full of days</b> — that his days were in honor and in joy." "The interpretation of the words of the book of Job is complete, and now I shall interpret the reasons. Job said: \"that which I dreaded has come upon me.\" Now Eliphaz thought that he did not dread [it], only because of a sin he had committed. And behold, he makes a general statement: when Job's friends saw the sudden death of all his sons, and fire descending from the heavens, and the plundering by his enemies, and the sores on his body, it became clear to them that HaShem does not inflict blows like these except upon sinners and transgressors, like the men of the generation of the Flood; therefore he said: \"a river pours out their foundation,\" and the men of Sodom and the rest fire consumed. Therefore they said to him: \"Remember now, who is innocent that perished?\" — \"Such are the ways of all who forget El.\" And Zophar said: \"the eyes of the wicked will fail.\" And because his sons were continually at a feast, and wine corrupts the mind, therefore he said: \"If your sons sinned against Him, He sent them away by the hand of their transgression.\" And they said: let him repent and HaShem will forgive him. And Job knew in himself that he was righteous; therefore he said: \"I am blameless, I do not know myself\"; \"without violence in my hands.\" And he cried out, for these blows that HaShem struck me are not on account of sin but gratuitously; therefore he said: \"and many wounds without cause.\" His friends answered this: that HaShem is righteous in all His ways; He does no injustice. Job answered and said: behold, in the world there are righteous and wicked in suffering — \"the blameless and the wicked He destroys\" — and there are wicked in pleasantness, and this is the meaning of \"they are at ease, tents for plunderers.\" His friends answered: the good of the wicked does not endure; \"if he soars to the heavens, his boast\" — \"like his dung he perishes forever.\" And behold Job answered: \"Why do the wicked live on, grow old, grow mighty in strength?\" \"They spend their days in goodness\" — \"and in an instant they go down to Sheol\" — and behold they are in goodness until the day of their death?! Then Bildad did not know what to answer, and behold he returned to praising HaShem: \" dominion and dread are with Him.\" Then Job answered: \"How have you helped the powerless?\" — He too praised HaShem, and the meaning is: behold, you have no reply but to praise HaShem; I know how to praise Him more. Then Zophar fell silent, and Job returned and continued to speak, because not one of his friends had answered him. And the meaning of \"earth, from which bread comes forth, and underneath it is turned over as by fire,\" and afterward \"the wisdom, from where is it found?\" — that HaShem does His will; He afflicts the wicked and also the righteous, and there are righteous and also wicked to whom He gives every good they desire, and behold He does so on earth and in wadis that have no knowledge. But to know this wisdom — \"why is it exalted beyond human knowledge?!\" And behold the meaning: since human beings do not know the thoughts of HaShem and His decrees, for they are deep, perhaps these decrees that He decreed upon me — He knows why He decreed them, and they are not because I sinned before Him. And Job continued his second parable: he recounted His righteousness and His deeds, for his friends said to him: \"you sent widows away empty,\" and this is a sign that he was a judge; therefore he said: \"I was a father to the needy, and the cause of the widow I would sing.\" And his friends ceased answering him, for he was righteous in his own eyes, though it was not so in his friends' eyes; rather their opinion concurred that he was wicked, therefore these harsh troubles came upon him suddenly. And Elihu grew angry at Job because he prolonged his tongue in his shortness of spirit, and said things against HaShem that were not fitting, such as: \"I know that You will bring me to death\"; \"inform me, why do You contend with me?\"; \"Is it good for You that You oppress, that You despise the work of Your hands?!\" And this is the meaning of \"on his righteousness before Elohim\" — and concerning his three friends he grew angry at their saying that HaShem is righteous but Job is wicked. And it is possible that he was righteous, and there is no doubt that HaShem is righteous, and this is the meaning of \"and they condemned Job.\" Now behold, the secret of Elihu's words, which he said to Job and his friends: all of you have erred, for you thought that you stood in the council of HaShem. And behold he said to Job's friends, that HaShem tests and afflicts on account of sin, or to remove sin, or to receive reward. And he said to Job, who was complaining against HaShem for not answering him — \"for all his words He does not answer\" — but the matter is openly revealed in deeds, yet Job seized upon it and said that He is wicked by the word of his mouth, for he expanded against HaShem. Likewise: \"He said, to a king of belial\" — and the meaning: the way of musar is that a servant should say to his king 'belial,' that is, wicked, or a king to his nobles, for a king serves himself first and then his people; or its meaning refers to one of the nobles, like \"the firstborn of his donkey.\" And the first interpretation is correct in my eyes, even though HaShem does not show favoritism to princes; therefore he said: \"My father will test Job to the end,\" and \"a path for the company of workers of iniquity.\" And lastly, \"Elihu continued\" — and the meaning is important, for behold, regarding the marvels of rain, one does not know how they are, nor why so, nor when. And just as these secrets are wondrous to us, so are the judgments of HaShem with human beings wondrous, for they are deeper than those that concern visible bodies. And the meaning regarding Job: \"He speaks\" — \"why did you raise your voice to say, 'Why did You answer me?' You should have endured and been silent and not spoken so high and lofty.\" And this is the meaning of \"HaShem answered from the storm,\" and the matter: you who seek \"why\" — \"did you know, then you would bring forth\" — and do you know how long you will live? And this light in which you delight — do you know its place? Or do you know the nature of birds, and likewise the beasts? And this is the meaning of \"Do you give strength to the horse?\" Then Job fell silent. HaShem answered a second time: \"Will one who contends with Shaddai be corrected?\" Then Job answered — a proper repentance it was not, for he did not justify HaShem, but said: \"Behold, I will be silent and speak no more\"; therefore \"HaShem answered a second time from the storm,\" and He mentioned Behemoth, which is the name of a beast, and its might, which is on dry land; and He also mentioned the might of Leviathan, and the meaning is as though He said to Job: how did you exalt yourself against Me? Therefore He mentioned these great beings, and the matter: behold, [I spoke] of My creations — and not among the exalted celestial beings, but of the beasts that are on earth and in the waters beneath the earth, two mighty beings that you cannot subjugate, nor can you draw out Leviathan to consume him. For a human being should not exalt his heart against the angels, nor against the sons of Elohim, for they are more exalted than him doubly, as I explained in the verse: \"Behold, I send an angel\" — with complete proofs drawn from reason and from the science of nature, and likewise the words of the prophets unanimously. And that which we find written in the words of our early Sages of blessed memory is also true, for they spoke only of the angels created at their time to honor the prophets; but to exalt one's heart against beasts and birds, as the preceding verse said: \"from the beasts of the earth.\" And behold HaShem said: behold, there are bodies on earth with you, and they are mightier than you; you cannot prevail against them — so why is your heart exalted? Therefore Job acknowledged at the end and said: \"Therefore I retract and console myself over dust and ashes.\" And this is the proper [understanding], that Job spoke at the end."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {1 "The bulk of this comment is Ibn Ezra's prose summary of the entire book's argument-flow; I have translated it as continuous text preserving his chain of verse-quotations and paratactic connectives without inserting explanatory bridge sentences. Throughout, divine names are transliterated per protocol."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:06:17.042111390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}